Doug,et alia:

The last Keyspan unit I had went to a customer (they needed it and =20
paid a premium to buy my personal unit, something I had been =20
preferential to avoid), and I never replaced it until I had an =20
emergent need for one again, at which point I got one of the Prolific =20
chipset based units. It works okay, and their drivers seem to be up to =
=20
date, but I have a bit more trust in Keyspan to persistently provision =
=20
MacOS drivers for newer variants of MacOS as time goes on.

One thing I am entirely interested in doing is getting a dual serial =20
port USB cable, so I can use it for monitoring serial communications =20
(data scope type functionality) between two devices (which I do have =20
occasion to do sometimes). There is a piece of software that does data =
=20
scope type functions (it is part of the "chirp" project for =20
programming D-STAR radios) that does compile and function within the =20
MacOS environment.

A unit from pimfg.com was recommended, but I never took the time to =20
verify its compatibility level as far as Linux and MacOS goes (I =20
always presume that these devices are Windows compliant for the most =20
part), though pimfg.com generally has excellent prices on Chinese =20
cable type stuff.

I was unaware of the multiport variants of the Keyspan, but I'd sure =20
like to pick one up or review the specifications for it. I had also =20
considered an FTDI based chipset device too, since their support =20
across platforms seem to be very pervasive.


V/R,

Stuart, N3GWG



